WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.270
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Bill Keller, who was the founding editor

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:09.260
in chief of the Marshall Project and also
author of the book What's Prison for Punishment

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.080
and Rehabilitation in the Age of Mass Incarceration.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.570
Bill, I really appreciate you being on today.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.259
Speaker 5: Thanks for having me.

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:23.340
Speaker 1: So, I mean, I guess to start with,
at least in some theoretical sense, prison

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.289
sentences in the United States are supposed
to be for rehabilitation.

00:00:27.289 --> 00:00:28.289
Right.

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:36.211
The idea that someone has done something wrong,
harmful to society and or to victims, they

00:00:36.211 --> 00:00:40.500
have been sentenced to some amount of time
in prison.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.180
And that that experience, the time that they
spend in prison is at least to some degree,

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.370
rehabilitative, in addition to being punitive,
so that when they serve their sentence, they

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.330
come out and now they're rehabilitated.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:54.330
Right.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:55.530
I mean, at least sort of theoretically.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:01:01.630
Can you talk about whether in practice any
elements of the criminal justice system in

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.439
the U.S. actually function in that way?

00:01:04.439 --> 00:01:07.149
Speaker 5: Not really.

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:12.350
I mean, you can find individual programs that
function that way.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:16.750
Some of them are experimental, some of them
more broadly based, like college education

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:20.100
programs that actually do rehabilitate.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.781
But there's there are a number of obstacles.

00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:24.869
One of them is cost.

00:01:24.869 --> 00:01:28.009
It's expensive to do rehabilitation programs.

00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:30.700
You know, you need trained personnel and so
on.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:37.999
One of them is puritanical streak, I think,
in American culture.

00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:43.819
The main objective from that point of view
and rehabilitation is just pampering.

00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:49.219
Also, prisons are not the most ideal therapeutic
environment.

00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:55.299
They're scary, they're noisy that people will
leave.

00:01:55.299 --> 00:02:00.560
Some are really cops, not therapists or social
workers.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.109
They don't have the training to administer
those sorts of programs.

00:02:04.109 --> 00:02:06.439
So there are a lot of obstacles.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:09.710
The heroes of my book, if I can call them
that, are the.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:16.629
The corrections officials are trying to improve
rehabilitation in spite of the obstacles and

00:02:16.629 --> 00:02:21.599
the teachers, the volunteers, the.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:26.930
Kind of a mystery why I chose to write about
write about prisons rather than courts or

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.269
cops because.

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:34.349
There's been a lot of attention, deservedly
paid, to policing judges and prosecutors,

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:39.230
for the most part run for public office and
do a lot of their business in public.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:42.060
Prisons are kind of a mystery.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.269
So that's that's why I wanted to focus on
that.

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:51.150
Speaker 1: If we put aside for a second when
there are actual rehabilitative rehabilitation

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:58.930
programs, the sort of status quo day to day
of being in your standard prison at this point,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.320
we know beyond any doubt that's not a rehabilitative
experience in any way, is it?

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.980
And in fact, that it could sort of be the
opposite.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:15.730
Speaker 5: Now we write there's considerable
research by people like Bruce Western at Columbia

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:20.990
who show that being incarcerated can actually
make you more likely to commit crimes when

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:22.140
you get out.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.140
Right.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:30.000
Speaker 1: And when we when we say that, I
mean, there's this sort of general idea.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.069
Prison can turn you into it, turn you into
a more hardened or violent criminal.

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:35.409
And it's often repeated.

00:03:35.409 --> 00:03:39.700
But what sort of what sort of data do we have
about that?

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:47.849
Speaker 5: Well, the one piece of data that's
kind of shocking is that.

00:03:47.849 --> 00:03:53.000
Two thirds of the people who are incarcerated
will be re-arrested within five years of their

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.000
release.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.239
The recidivism rate is a tricky metric, and
then people measure it different ways.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:04.380
But recidivism suggests that we are not doing
a very effective job of rehabilitating overall.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:12.859
Speaker 1: When we talk about the deterrent
effect of the system that we have and the

00:04:12.859 --> 00:04:20.579
idea that at the end of some crime that one
commits, if you are caught and charged and

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:23.190
convicted, there could be a punishment.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.250
That's coming from what I've read.

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:33.060
Studies have found that the death penalty,
as compared to life in prison without parole,

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:38.699
does not seem to be a functional deterrent
in terms of reducing the crimes wherein one

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:42.139
would be eligible for such punishments.

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:48.910
What do we know more generally about prison
sentences and the deterrent effect on those

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:51.020
not in prison?

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:56.450
Speaker 5: There's a sociologist named Francis
Cullen at University of Cincinnati who's done

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.580
a lot of work on this subject.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:03.860
And he he makes this logical argument that
the deterrent effect assumes that before you

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:09.550
commit a crime, you stop and consider the
risks in a sort of logical way.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:15.479
Whereas the great majority of crimes are either
opportunistic that are done in concert with,

00:05:15.479 --> 00:05:20.000
you know, going along with a gang.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.000
They're spontaneous.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.310
And to the extent that people do stop and
consider the consequences before they do something

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:28.910
illegal, then they're mainly considering the
chances of getting caught.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:29.910
Right.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:34.860
It doesn't make a difference to them whether
the judgment is five years for a felony or

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.270
20 years for a felony.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.280
But that's just not the way people think.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.360
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.300
You know, we were talking about jaywalking
yesterday on our program because the California

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:51.220
Governor Newsom recently signed into law a
bill that that mostly decriminalized this

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.360
jaywalking.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:56.590
And I think that there's sort of some gray
area in the sense that, like, you know, I

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:58.240
jaywalk all the time.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:05.919
And it is true that if I knew that life in
prison was a possible sentence for jaywalking,

00:06:05.919 --> 00:06:07.060
I wouldn't do it.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:12.840
But you need these really extreme examples
that that in order to really be able to make

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:13.840
that case.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.690
Whereas as you talk about with many crimes,
murder in particular, murders are are such

00:06:18.690 --> 00:06:19.889
a type of crime.

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:24.979
We're already you're talking about only a
fraction of the population is even willing

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:26.250
to commit a murder.

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:30.830
Based on the research we have that the line
between life in prison with no parole and

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:35.260
death penalty, for example, it seems like
it's a completely different type of consideration

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.400
than my jaywalking example.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.139
Speaker 5: Right.

00:06:39.139 --> 00:06:45.440
I mean, I would never say that that the risk
of going to jail or being severely punished

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.680
doesn't figure into anybody's thinking at
all.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:55.879
And the deterrent exists, but it's just greatly
exaggerated when you weigh that against the

00:06:55.879 --> 00:07:04.320
the effects of being incarcerated on commit
another crime.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.360
It's not it's not a convincing reason to lock
people up, especially to lock people up as

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:13.550
we do for far longer periods of time and for
lesser crimes than most other countries.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.199
Speaker 1: Yeah, I want to talk about that
next.

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:23.830
So can you give some specific examples of
average sentence in the U.S. for certain crimes

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:29.340
compared to in some other countries for which
we have data for the same crime?

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:32.979
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:07:32.979 --> 00:07:34.190
Sorry.

00:07:34.190 --> 00:07:36.379
I just lost my train of thought.

00:07:36.379 --> 00:07:37.379
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:07:37.379 --> 00:07:38.750
Any crime that you.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:44.180
That we could think of a difference in average
sentencing between the U.S. and other countries?

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:45.780
Speaker 5: Oh, I know.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:52.030
What I was going to say is mass incarceration
is a relatively new phenomenon for us.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:08:00.930
I think that's important to remember that
for most of the 20th century.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:06.840
The incarceration rate in the United States
was something like 100 people incarcerated

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:11.199
for every 100,000 population, which was a
little higher than the rest of the world,

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:13.930
but not not off the tracks.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:18.160
And then beginning in the seventies, we took
a sharp right turn.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:19.650
And it became more like 500.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.550
People over 100,000, the population.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:26.740
I mean, that's an incredible phenomenon.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.389
And part of that is.

00:08:29.389 --> 00:08:34.690
That we incarcerate people for much longer
periods of time most.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:38.610
I'm talking here about developed countries,
mostly the European countries, the ones that

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.829
I take a look at.

00:08:40.829 --> 00:08:42.260
Most countries don't have a death penalty.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:45.040
They don't have life without parole.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:46.079
And.

00:08:46.079 --> 00:08:54.860
Look, I talk about the case of Anders Breivik,
the Norwegian mass murderer who slaughtered

00:08:54.860 --> 00:09:01.230
70 some kids at a party political party picnic.

00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:06.690
And instead of 21 years now, it is deemed
to be a danger to society.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:08.550
A judge can extend that.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:13.680
But that's that's a severe plus to maximum
punishment.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:14.680
Right.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:15.940
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:23.430
Are there you know, sometimes there's this
idea that if you do the European style of

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:30.589
incarceration where conditions generally speaking
are better and sentences are shorter, that

00:09:30.589 --> 00:09:32.560
there is a higher recidivism rate.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:35.300
But it doesn't appear to be borne out in the
data, is it?

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:36.300
Speaker 5: No.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:41.490
The recidivism rates in all of the Scandinavian
countries and Germany and most of the developed

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:45.020
world are considerably lower than ours.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:50.670
Speaker 1: So to me, what that says is that
for some of the folks in the United States

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:57.870
who are demanding the stricter prison sentences,
I don't really think they care, Bill, about

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.570
the rehabilitative effect or the the odds
of recidivism.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:08.070
I think there's a sort of you did the thing
and now you deserve a strict punishment.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:12.750
There's kind of this strict father morality
aspect to it that's totally untethered from

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.529
the actual impact.

00:10:14.529 --> 00:10:15.529
What's your sense?

00:10:15.529 --> 00:10:17.850
Speaker 5: That's absolutely right.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:21.230
There are a couple of reasons why we should
care.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:25.470
Even a lot of people have a hard sell on it.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:31.250
There's a, I guess, moral reason that Americans
like to profess that we believe in second

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:34.450
chances and redemption.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:37.280
And so if we want to live up to those ideals,
we should be.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:42.610
Treating them as if they were redeemable.

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:49.870
But beyond that, there's a pragmatic, down
to earth, self centered argument for rehabilitation.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:55.410
Every year we let out 600,000 people from
state and federal prisons.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:11:04.320
Would you rather have those people emerge
from prison, brutalized, alienated, stigmatized

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:08.140
and lacking in any kind of skills that will
help them reenter the real world?

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:12.500
Or would you like to have them equipped with
citizens and neighbors?

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:15.570
I'd made a pretty convincing argument.

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:22.120
Speaker 1: Yeah, it seems the preferable answer
is clear on that.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:28.970
To talk a little bit about the kind of society
that is created within many prisons, you know,

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:34.639
I, I interviewed the author, Ted Conover,
who wrote this book, New Jack, in which he

00:11:34.639 --> 00:11:36.779
actually became a great book.

00:11:36.779 --> 00:11:43.420
He became a corrections officer at Sing Sing
in New York and talks about his experiences.

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:47.010
And he did it knowing his plan was ultimately
to write a book.

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:53.880
And he talks quite a bit about the reality
of the sort of culture around being a corrections

00:11:53.880 --> 00:12:00.199
officer in the United States and the pressures
faced systemic and and otherwise.

00:12:00.199 --> 00:12:06.971
And he doesn't excuse any of the behavior
that he observed when he was in the job, but

00:12:06.971 --> 00:12:12.970
he sort of gives a glimpse into the realities
of how the corrections officers circumstances

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:14.860
impact how prisons are run as well.

00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:16.660
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:21.880
What is unique in the United States about
being a corrections officer and the expectations

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:23.360
and the pressures?

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:30.310
Speaker 5: If you want to be a corrections
officer in Germany, you have to take two years

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:38.920
of college level courses, including things
like human rights, law, psychology, sociology.

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:40.990
And then you're.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:47.680
Your job is to be a therapist is putting it
a little too strongly, but to engage the inmates,

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:54.480
to try to figure out what it was that made
them go wrong and see what programs would

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.710
be able to address it.

00:12:56.710 --> 00:13:00.050
If you're going to become a corrections officer
in the United States, and I'm generalizing

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:05.149
broadly here, there's a saying in corrections
that you've seen one prison.

00:13:05.149 --> 00:13:06.710
You've seen one prison.

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:08.880
Everyone has its own kind of culture.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:10.000
Yeah.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.710
But for the most part, you're going to become
a corrections officer, as Ted Conover's book

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:14.710
demonstrated.

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:20.950
You're going to get a few weeks of training,
mostly about crowd control and self-defense.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:24.829
It's it's a it's more of a paramilitary organization.

00:13:24.829 --> 00:13:33.231
It's I have as Ted emerged from that experience
with a fair amount of empathy for the prison

00:13:33.231 --> 00:13:38.380
guards, that they're, in a sense, victims
of the environment and the culture, too.

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:41.090
And I think there's some truth in that.

00:13:41.090 --> 00:13:42.670
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:44.060
And it seems to some degree.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:45.060
Oh, sorry, Bill.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:51.190
I was going to say there seems to be a mirroring
in one of the things that it seems many police

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:56.010
departments would benefit from mirrors something
that corrections officers would benefit from

00:13:56.010 --> 00:14:00.350
as well, which is more training along the
lines of of sort of social work and relationship

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:02.170
building and community building.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:04.529
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:14:04.529 --> 00:14:07.009
And you can find leaders.

00:14:07.009 --> 00:14:14.370
Speaker 5: Of organized corrections officers,
unions and things who will say that it's.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:19.649
In the last ten years or so, they've begun
to talk the talk a little bit.

00:14:19.649 --> 00:14:24.200
Unfortunately, they're organized and in the
states that have really strong corrections

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:30.199
officers, unions, they tend to be a source
of resistance because that's.

00:14:30.199 --> 00:14:37.880
Speaker 1: One of the things that is more
unique in the United States is this concept

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:44.350
of a for profit prison run by a corporation
that contracts with various agencies.

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:49.959
And sometimes those contracts include a guaranteed
occupancy where even if there is not actually

00:14:49.959 --> 00:14:54.300
a certain occupancy, the prison operator gets
paid as if they were.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:59.100
So they have a certain expectation of guaranteed
revenue that they are going to be receiving.

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:03.680
Can you talk a little bit about even though
most inmates in the United States are not

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:10.370
held at for profit private prisons, what is
particular or unique to the for profit prison

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:12.300
experience in the United States?

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:15.850
Or is it the same as in these state run and
federal prisons?

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:22.300
Speaker 5: You know, there's another book
by a journalist who went undercover as a prison

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:25.769
guard to Shane Bauer's book.

00:15:25.769 --> 00:15:31.319
He went underground in a Louisiana prison
that was a private by Corrections Corporation

00:15:31.319 --> 00:15:32.829
of America.

00:15:32.829 --> 00:15:38.670
And he he does a nice job, first of all, of
demonstrating that the bottom line becomes

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:43.610
a big factor in the thinking of those prisons.

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:47.899
But also the whole history of incarceration
in America.

00:15:47.899 --> 00:15:52.810
It has various profit motives associated with
starting those and most egregiously, with

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:56.830
the South, where prison labor became a substitute
for slave labor.

00:15:56.830 --> 00:15:57.830
Hmm.

00:15:57.830 --> 00:16:02.070
With much improvement in the conditions and
certainly without any pay.

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:10.579
Speaker 1: So it seems as though it's essentially
quite similar, but with the profit motive

00:16:10.579 --> 00:16:11.861
added on top of it.

00:16:11.861 --> 00:16:13.610
I mean, is that fair to say?

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:17.560
Speaker 5: Yeah, that's that's I think that's
fair to say.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:22.940
It's actually you're right in saying that
it's a small percentage of the incarcerated

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:30.770
population who are in privately run prisons,
but that misses auxiliary services, that even

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:40.200
the state run prisons contract for people
to make available to to do medical care, to

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:43.040
do transport, to run the cafeterias.

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:45.970
Often those are contracted out to private.

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:48.730
Companies that don't have any incentive to
do.

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:51.759
Speaker 3: More than they have to.

00:16:51.759 --> 00:16:56.570
Speaker 1: Also, there's elements of privatization
even within state and federally run prisons

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:57.570
as well.

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:58.570
I did not know that.

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:00.300
Speaker 3: Well.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:07.450
Speaker 5: One example that's been a big issue
lately in a lot of prisons is there's a company

00:17:07.450 --> 00:17:18.530
that makes available tablets so that you can
download books for your class and communicate.

